# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The LlamaAccount contract will be destroyed and all the assets can be lost.

## Proof of Concept
In execute(), we use _readSlot0() to prevent a malicious (or buggy) target from taking ownership of this contract. But the malicious target can send all the assets and destroy itself, making this check useless.

The attacker can deploy an upgradable contract first, which doesn't contain selfdestruct and other malicious instructions. When the execute action is queued and ready to be trigged, he upgrades the innocent contract to a selfdestruct contract, thus taking all the assets.

I wrote a code snippet to show the checks in execute() are useless for selfdestruct:

```solidity
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity 0.8.19;

contract B {
    uint public num;

    function setVars(uint _num) public payable {
        num = _num;
        selfdestruct(payable(0)); 
    }
}

contract A {
    uint public num;

    function setVars(address _contract, uint _num) public payable returns(uint) {
        (bool success,) = _contract.delegatecall(
            abi.encodeWithSignature("setVars(uint256)", _num)
        );
        require(success, "NO success!");
        return 1;
    }
}
```

A call to A.setVars(B, 123) will make A destroyed.

## Tools Used
Manual review.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Arbitrary delegatecall is dangerous for an assets contract. Please consider using call only.






## Assessed type

call/delegatecall

